<div id="title">Fenwick Tree / Binary Indexed Tree (BIT)</div>
<p class="text-small"><i>Note: the description and implementation shown below will be for sum queries and single updates only. Other versions of Fenwick trees exist.</i></p>
<br>
<p>
    A Fenwick Tree or Binary Indexed Tree (BIT) is a data structure that stores information about prefix sums in an array that allows for efficent updating of elements by
    re-calculating the prefix sum in <b>O(log(n))</b> time.
</p>
<br>
<p>
    It is an implict tree, meaning that it is actually stored as an array, where parent-child relationships are determined by running calculations on indexes to find
    their parent.
</p>
<p class="header">Array Structure</p>
<p>
    Given an array <var>A</var> of length <var>N</var>, the size of the Fenwick Tree will be <var>N</var> + 1 as the 0th index is a dummy, and stores the sum of the range
    <var>A(0, 0]</var>. Each odd index in the Fenwick tree will store the sum of the range <var>A(i - 1, i]</var> which in practise is the same as <var>A[i - 1]</var>.
    Then the remaining odd indexes will store the sum of the range <var>A(i - 2, i]</var>. Then the remaining odd indexes will store the sum of the range 
    <var>A(i - 4, i]</var>, and carrying on this process until there are no more empty indexes left.
</p>
<p class="header-small">Example</p>
<p>Given an array <var>A</var> of length <var>N</var></p>
<p class="code-block">[5, 13, -4, 12, 7, 0, -11, 8]</p>
<p>The corresponding Fenwick Tree <var>F</var> will look like</p>
<p class="code-block">[0, 5, 18, -4, 26, 7, 7, -11, 30]</p>
<p>One way to write this is by saying <var>F[i]</var> = <var>A(i - lsb(i), i]</var>.</p>
<p>The function <var>lsb</var> returns the value of the smallest set bit of a number.</p>
<p class="text-small"><var>lsb(13)</var> == <var>lsb(1101<sub>2</sub>)</var> == 1, and <var>lsb(24)</var> == <var>lsb(11000<sub>2</sub>)</var> == 8</p>
<p class="header">Populating The Array</p>
<p>Given an array <var>Arr</var> initalise an array <var>F</var> of length <var>N</var> + 1 filled with 0s.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">F = [0] * (N + 1)</code></pre>
<p>Next, we need to iterate over the range (1, <var>N</var>) and set a variable <var>r</var> to <var>i</var>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">for i in range(1, N + 1):
    r = i
</code></pre>
<p>
    Now we can add <var>arr[i]</var> to <var>F[r]</var> and then set <var>r</var> to <var>r + (r & -r)</var>, which is equal to <var>r + lsb(r)</var>. By doing this
    <var>r</var> becomes the parent index of the previous value of <var>r</var> allowing us to traverse the implicit tree.
</p>
<p>We want to repeat the prior 2 steps until the value of <var>r</var> is greater than <var>N</var>, the whole code looks like this.</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">def populate(arr):
    N = len(arr)
    F = [0] * (N + 1)

    for i in range(1, N + 1):
        r = i
        while r <= N:
            F[r] += arr[i - 1]
            r += (r & -r)  # r = r + lsb(r)
</code></pre>
<p>
    A more efficent version of this exists, where we can just add <var>F[r]</var> to <var>F[r + (r & -r)</var>. By passing along the current value, we pass down the
    running sum instead of each individual value. The code for it looks like this.
</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">def populate(arr):
    N = len(arr)
    F = [0] * (N + 1)

    for i in range(1, N + 1):
        r = i + (i & -i)  # r = i + lsb(i)
        if r <= N: F[r] += F[i]
</code></pre>
<p>This code runs in <b>O(n)</b> time instead of <b>O(nlog(n))</b> time.</p>
<p class="header">Updating The Array</p>
<p>Updating the array is very easy as populating the array is essentially updating the array at each index in <var>arr</var>.</p>
<p>
    Given a value <var>val</var> and an index <var>idx</var>, we find the difference between <var>val</var> and the current value at <var>idx</var> and call it
    <var>delta</var>, then we set the value at <var>arr[idx]</var> to <var>val</var>.
</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">delta = val - arr[index]</code></pre>
<p>
    Next, we start at <var>idx</var> + 1 and traverse the Fenwick tree like we did when populating the array, and adding <var>delta</var> to each index, the code
    looks like this.
</p>
