<p class="header">Finding The Range Sum</p>
<p>
    Much like a usual prefix sum array, given two indexes <var>left</var> and <var>right</var>, we can find the sum in a range by subtracting the left sum from
    the right sum. But how do we find the left and right sums in the first place?
</p>
<br>
<p>
    Instead of jumping to the next index in which our current range in contained, we want to jump to the index that starts our range. The way to do this is to subtract
    the lsb of out current index until we get to 0, and summing all the values at each traversed index. This works because the range for a given index in <var>F</var>
    can be written as <var>arr(i - lsb(i), i]</var>, and each index in <var>F</var> always ends their range with <var>i</var>.
</p>
<br>
<p>First, we want to initalise a variable <var>sum</var> to keep track of the running sum</p>
<p>
    Next, we want to add the value at <var>F[i]</var> to <var>sum</var> and set the value of <var>i</var> to <var>i & (i - 1) </var>, which is equal to <var>i - lsb(i)</var>.
    We want to repeat the last two steps until the value of <var>i</var> is equal to 0, in which case we can return <var>sum</var>. The code looks like this.
</p>
<pre><code class="language-python">def prefixSum(i):
    sum = 0
    while i > 0:
        sum += F[i]
        i -= (i & -i) # i = i - lsb(i)
    return sum

def rangeSum(left, right):
    return prefixSum(right) - prefixSum(left)
</code></pre>